CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a four-column visualization of the activations inside a small neural network, the columns arranged left to right as successive stages of training. Each column is a vertical stack of seven flat square planes in isometric perspective, one plane per layer output, joined by dashed trajectory curves that carry every datapoint from one plane to the next. Points appear in two colors, red and blue. In all four columns the bottom plane shows the two colors thoroughly intermingled in a dense central cluster, and moving upward they are pulled apart, the red trajectories drawing into a tight vertical bundle while the blue ones bow outward to one side. A legend column at the far left gives the architecture as a bottom-to-top chain of arrows and rounded labels reading linear, relu, linear, relu, linear, and softmax. Beneath the panels a long horizontal arrow labeled Training iteration runs the full width, with sets of three dots between columns marking skipped iterations. Reading up a column shows how one forward pass reshapes the data distribution layer by layer; reading across shows the separation growing cleaner and the blue points swinging farther out as training proceeds.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Stack of 7 flat square planes (wireframe outlines, dark gray) arranged vertically, one per layer output: input, linear, relu, linear, relu, linear, softmax
  - Faint gray grid lines subdividing each square plane
  - Scatter of ~120 datapoints on each plane, half red (#e8262a) and half blue (#1a35e8), plotted as small filled dots
  - Dashed trajectory curves connecting each datapoint on one plane to the same datapoint on the plane above, colored by class, fading toward the far end
  - Thin gray dashed 'bounding' lines connecting the four corners of consecutive planes, forming the prism silhouette of the stack
  - Left-hand architecture legend column: vertical chain of upward arrows alternating with rounded rectangle labels reading (bottom to top) linear, relu, linear, relu, linear, softmax
  - Bottom horizontal arrow spanning the full width labeled 'Training iteration'
  - Ellipsis marks (three dots) along the training-iteration axis indicating skipped iterations
  - Iteration readout / tick marks under the training arrow marking the four sampled snapshots

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
One column only by default. Seven square planes, each 4 × 4 world units lying in the XZ plane (X = screen-right-ish, Z = receding diagonal), stacked along +Y at spacing 2.2 units, so plane centers at y = 0, 2.2, 4.4, 6.6, 8.8, 11.0, 13.2; total stack height ~13.2 units. Bottom plane (y=0) = input data; then linear-1 (2.2), relu-1 (4.4), linear-2 (6.6), relu-2 (8.8), linear-3 (11.0), softmax (13.2) — reading bottom to top, matching the left legend. Each plane drawn as a dark-gray outline (#444444, ~1.5px) with a faint interior 4×4 grid (#cccccc). ~60 red points (#E8262A) and ~60 blue points (#1A35E8), dot radius ~0.045 units. Trajectories: dashed cubic curves, dash ~0.12/0.10, opacity 0.35-0.6, colored by class, drawn from each point's position on plane k to its position on plane k+1. Corner-to-corner gray dashed prism lines (#999999, opacity 0.5) join the four corners of consecutive planes. Data model: input distribution is two interleaved 2D spirals/rings centered at the plane origin with radius up to ~1.2, red and blue fully intermingled (this bottom layout NEVER changes with the iteration slider). Upper-plane positions are produced by a small MLP whose weights interpolate with the iteration slider t in [0,1]: at t=0 weights are random and the cloud stays mixed and roughly centered; at t=1 red points collapse into a tight bundle of radius <0.15 near the plane center/one corner while blue points fan outward, some blue trajectories overshooting well beyond the plane edge (up to ~4 units off to the +X side, as in the rightmost source column). Overshoot magnitude grows monotonically with t. Softmax plane positions must lie on the 2-simplex: clamp to the unit triangle so late-training points sit at corners. Legend column sits to the LEFT of the stack at x ≈ -5, drawn as a 2D overlay: six rounded-rect labels in monospace ('linear','relu','linear','relu','linear','softmax' bottom to top) alternating with black up-arrows, vertically aligned with the gaps between planes. Bottom overlay: horizontal black arrow with 'Training iteration' label at the left end and four tick marks with ellipses between them, mapped to the slider value. Background white.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "trainingIteration",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "Optimization progress is the same thing as growing class separability: as the iteration count rises the red and blue point clouds, which start fully intermingled at the bottom plane, get pulled into a tight red bundle and an outward-swinging blue arc in the upper planes.",
    "label": "Training iteration",
    "range": [
      0,
      3000,
      10
    ],
    "default": 0
  },
  {
    "id": "layerStep",
    "type": "state",
    "teaches": "Each layer reshapes the very same set of points: stepping upward through linear → relu → linear → relu → linear → softmax highlights one plane and the incoming trajectories, showing which operation does the stretching (linear), which does the folding/clipping (relu), and which does the final squashing onto the class simplex (softmax).",
    "label": "Forward pass, layer by layer",
    "states": [
      "input",
      "linear-1",
      "relu-1",
      "linear-2",
      "relu-2",
      "linear-3",
      "softmax",
      "all"
    ],
    "default": "all"
  },
  {
    "id": "traceDatapoint",
    "type": "hover",
    "teaches": "The dashed curves are one continuous path per datapoint: hovering a single red or blue dot brightens its trajectory through all seven planes, making the continuity between layerwise transformations and its arrival in the final class bundle explicit."
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitStack",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The planes are genuine 2D activation spaces stacked in depth, not a flat 2D drawing; rotating shows the red bundle and blue arc occupy different regions of each plane rather than merely overlapping on screen."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Untrained network",
    "narration": "At iteration 0 the red and blue points stay mixed all the way up the stack — every plane looks like the tangled input cluster, so the network cannot separate the classes.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 0,
      "layerStep": "all"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Step through one forward pass",
    "narration": "Walk upward one layer at a time. The linear layers stretch and rotate the cloud; the relu layers fold points against the axes; nothing yet pulls the colors apart.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 0,
      "layerStep": "relu-1"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Mid-training",
    "narration": "Partway through training the upper planes begin to separate: red trajectories tighten toward a central bundle while blue ones start bowing out to one side.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 900,
      "layerStep": "all"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The softmax plane",
    "narration": "Highlighting only the top plane shows where the decision actually lands — points are driven toward the corners of the probability simplex.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 1800,
      "layerStep": "softmax"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Late training",
    "narration": "By the final iteration the bottom plane is still fully intermingled, but the stack above it has cleanly split the classes: a tight vertical red bundle and blue trajectories swinging far out. Separation is learned, not present in the data layout.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 3000,
      "layerStep": "all"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.72
}

NOTES: Render planes as LineSegments grids + an outline loop, not filled meshes, so trajectories remain visible through the stack. Use THREE.Line2/LineMaterial or a dashed LineDashedMaterial (call computeLineDistances) for the trajectories; batch all same-class curves into one merged geometry with per-vertex alpha to keep draw calls low (~120 curves × 6 segments). Precompute point positions for ~40 iteration keyframes and lerp between adjacent keyframes as the slider moves, rather than running a live MLP each frame. Hover picking: maintain an invisible THREE.Points cloud per plane with a raycaster threshold ~0.08; on hit, set a uniform highlightIndex that boosts that datapoint's curve alpha to 1.0 and lineWidth, while dimming all others to ~0.08. layerStep dims all planes/segments except the active one and its incoming trajectory bundle (active plane outline turns near-black and thickens). Isometric camera via OrthographicCamera at the standard axonometric pose; orbit should be damped and clamped in elevation so the stack never reads as a flat line. Legend, training-iteration arrow, and ellipses are best done as an HTML/CSS or 2D-canvas overlay pinned to the stack's screen-space y positions rather than as 3D text.

Follow the interaction plan above.

